---
title: "Interactive and explorable documents"
description: "Documents a reader can operate: reactive js cells, inputs without boilerplate, and 3-D."
---

Taliesin runs `{js}` cells in the browser and wires them into a small reactive
graph. On top of that graph sits one declarative convenience, inputs. Everything
here is client-side and offline, built from the same block model as the rest of
the page, and the preview stays read-only: a reader interacts with the output,
never the source.

The examples below are shown as source listings. Each feature has a runnable
counterpart in the corpus (`corpus/reactive/`, `corpus/descent/`, `corpus/layout/`).

## Reactive `{js}` cells

A `{js}` cell declares its place in a dependency graph with leading `//|`
directives (they must come before any code):

- `//| viewof: NAME` makes the cell an input control published under `NAME`.
- `//| name: NAME` publishes the cell's return value under `NAME`.
- `//| input: A, B` consumes those names; the cell re-runs when any of them change.

Inside a cell, read values through the injected `tali` API: `tali.value("k")` returns
a control's current value, and `tali.get("squared")` returns the last value a
`//| name` cell published. A first cell publishes a slider as `n`:

```js
//| viewof: n
const input = document.createElement("input");
input.type = "range"; input.min = "1"; input.max = "12"; input.value = "3";
return input;
```

A second derives a value from it, and a third consumes that derived value, so the
chain `n` to `squared` to the readout stays in sync transitively:

```js
//| name: squared
//| input: n
return tali.value("n") ** 2;
```

```js
//| input: squared
return document.createTextNode("n squared = " + tali.get("squared"));
```

When an input changes, Taliesin re-runs exactly the cells downstream of it, once
each, in dependency order, following `//| name` edges. This is one controlled pass,
not a continuously-recomputing dataflow engine: cycles are detected and shown as an
error rather than run. `Plot` and `d3` are available as globals for drawing; a cell
returns a DOM node (mounted for you) or builds into `tali.container`, and an
`invalidation` promise lets long-running cells (animations, WebGL) tear down before
a re-run.

A `viewof` input doesn't need to flow through a `//| name` cell first: a consumer
can list it directly in `//| input:`. Drag the slider to add odd harmonics to a
square-wave Fourier synthesis and watch the curve sharpen:

```js
//| viewof: harmonics
const wrap = document.createElement("label");
wrap.append("Harmonics ");
const input = document.createElement("input");
input.type = "range";
input.min = "1"; input.max = "12"; input.step = "1"; input.value = "3";
wrap.append(input);
return wrap;
```

```js
//| input: harmonics
const harmonics = tali.value("harmonics");
return Plot.lineY(
  Array.from({length: 500}, (_, t) =>
    d3.range(1, harmonics + 1).reduce(
      (sum, k) => sum + Math.sin(2 * Math.PI * (2 * k - 1) * t / 500) / (2 * k - 1), 0)),
  {curve: "basis", stroke: "#4c8dff"}
).plot({height: 200, marginLeft: 36, y: {label: "amplitude"}});
```

## Inputs without boilerplate: `{{< input >}}`

The `{{< input >}}` shortcode emits a labelled, keyboard-accessible control whose
value becomes a reactive node, so a `{js}` cell reacts with `//| input:` and no
control-building code. Five of them are ordinary form fields:

```markdown
{{< input name="k" type="slider" min="1" max="10" step="1" value="3" label="k" >}}
{{< input name="n" type="number" min="0" max="100" value="20" label="n" >}}
{{< input name="on" type="checkbox" value="true" label="enabled" >}}
{{< input name="q" type="text" value="hello" label="query" >}}
{{< input name="c" type="select" options="alpha,beta,gamma" value="beta" label="channel" >}}
```

| Argument | Required | Default | Effect |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name=` | warns if omitted | (none) | The reactive node's name (what a consuming cell reads). Omitting it warns (the control still renders, but it can't feed the reactive graph). |
| `type=` | no | `slider` | One of `slider`, `number`, `checkbox`, `text`, `select`. An unknown type warns with a did-you-mean. |
| `label=` | no | the `name` | The visible label text. |
| `value=` | no | (none) | The initial value. A `checkbox` is pre-checked only when `value` is the literal `true`; a `select`'s value must exactly string-match one of the `options` to pre-select it. |
| `min=` `max=` `step=` | no | (none) | Numeric bounds for `slider` and `number`. |
| `options=` | for `select` | (none) | A comma-separated option list. **Required** for `type="select"`; omitting it warns. |

`tali.value()` returns a number for `slider` and `number`, a boolean for
`checkbox`, and a string for `text` and `select`. A consuming cell is then just a
`{js}` cell with `//| input: k` that reads `tali.value("k")`.

A **`slider`** renders as HTML's `<input type="range">` with a live `<output>` readout
that updates as you drag, so the reader always sees the current value beside the track.
`number` shares its numeric `min`/`max`/`step`/`value` attributes but renders as a spinner
with no readout.

A **`select`** gives a closed set of choices, picked from the `options=` list.
`value` picks the initially selected option:

```markdown
{{< input name="palette" type="select" options="viridis,magma,cividis" value="magma" label="Palette" >}}
```

That publishes a reactive node `palette` whose value is the chosen string. A chart cell
then reacts to it:

```js
//| input: palette
const name = tali.value("palette");          // "viridis" | "magma" | "cividis"
return Plot.plot({ color: { scheme: name }, marks: [/* … */] });
```

## Interactive 3-D with a `{js}` cell

A `{js}` cell mounts whatever DOM node it returns, and that node can be a WebGL
canvas. So a full interactive 3-D scene, spin, zoom, fullscreen, is just a cell that
imports a 3-D library and returns its renderer's canvas. The shape is always the same:

```js
const THREE = await import("https://esm.sh/three@0.163.0");

const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true });
renderer.setSize(620, 500);

const scene  = new THREE.Scene();
const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(45, 620 / 500, 0.1, 100);
camera.position.set(4, 3, 6);
scene.add(new THREE.AmbientLight(0xffffff, 1));
scene.add(new THREE.Mesh(
  new THREE.IcosahedronGeometry(1.5, 0),
  new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x4c8dff, flatShading: true }),
));

(function animate() {
  requestAnimationFrame(animate);
  renderer.render(scene, camera);
})();

invalidation.then(() => renderer.dispose());   // clean up when the cell re-runs

return renderer.domElement;   // the cell mounts the canvas
```

Two things make this idiomatic. `invalidation` is a promise the cell provides that
resolves when the cell is about to re-run or leave the page, so it is where you cancel
animation frames and dispose GPU resources. And because a cell returns a node rather
than printing markup, you can attach controls, an `OrbitControls` from three.js, a
`<select>`, sliders, straight to the same container.

::: {.callout-warning}
## Gate teardown on `invalidation`, never on DOM attachment

The node a cell returns is mounted **after** the cell body finishes. Inside the body it
is not yet in the document, so a guard like this silently does nothing on the first
paint:

```js
const node = document.createElement("div");
if (!node.isConnected) return;      // always true here — the cell never renders
```

That reads like a defensive check and behaves like an early return. The same mistake
appears as an init gated on `offsetWidth`, on `getBoundingClientRect()`, or on a
`ResizeObserver` that has not fired yet: none of them has a useful answer until the node
is mounted, and none of them is how you learn the cell is finished. `invalidation` is,
and it works whether or not the node ever reaches the DOM. If you genuinely need
post-mount measurements (a canvas sized to its container), take them inside a
`requestAnimationFrame` callback, which runs after the mount.
:::

The data for a scene like this can come from a `{python}` cell instead of being
computed in the browser: compute coordinates or a mesh in the kernel and hand
them to the `{js}` cell that renders them. That handoff is its own pattern,
covered next.

## The Python → JS bridge

`define()` in a Python cell hands values to `{js}` cells, so you can **compute
in Python and render reactively in the browser**. Its signature is keyword-only,
`define(name=value, ...)`: each keyword becomes a published name, and each
value is JSON-serialized into the page for the `{js}` runtime to pick up. A `{js}`
cell then reads the value as `tali.defines.NAME` (or `tali.value("NAME")`, which
falls back to defines when no live input owns that name). In live preview the
Python cell executes *after* the page loads, so its values arrive a moment after
the `{js}` cells; a `{js}` cell reads them and re-runs automatically when they land
(and rebinds when a Python input changes), so the cell recomputes in place without
a reload. Guard against the value not having arrived yet with an early `return`.

```python
# sample a noisy sine wave
import math, random
random.seed(7)
signal = [math.sin(2 * math.pi * i / 40) + random.uniform(-0.3, 0.3) for i in range(120)]
define(signal=signal)
```

```js
const signal = tali.defines.signal;
if (!signal) return;
return Plot.plot({
  height: 180, marginLeft: 36,
  marks: [Plot.ruleY([0]), Plot.lineY(signal, {stroke: "#2bb673"})]
});
```

The landing page's rotating surface is built exactly this way, and so is the PCA
explainer in the sample blog: a `{js}` cell in a plain `.tmd` file, reading numbers a
`{python}` cell computed.
